Research spike #4, follow-up to inverse-decimation null-test refutation.

The multiplicity test asked: which homogeneous space G/H produces
eigenvalue multiplicities matching SM gauge irreps {1, 2, 3, 6, 8}?

Results (first 15 eigenvalues per space, multiplicity coverage vs
SM fermion content {1, 2, 3, 6}):

  S²:                {1, 3}        missing {2, 6}
  S³:                {1}           missing {2, 3, 6}
  CP²:               {1, 3, 6}     missing {2}    ← famous SU(2) hole
  S² × S²:           {1, 6}        missing {2, 3}
  S² × S¹:           {1, 3}        missing {2, 6}
  SU(2):             {1, 2, 3, 6}  perfect — but trivial
  SU(3):             {1, 3, 6}     missing {2}
  SU(3)×SU(2)×U(1):  {1, 2, 3, 6}  perfect — but tautological (SM gauge group)

Interpretation. SU(2)'s irrep dimensions are {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, ...} =
all naturals, so it trivially covers any target subset. The full SM
gauge manifold covers SM multiplicities BY CONSTRUCTION — that's the
definition of the SM gauge group. So the "perfect matches" do not
discriminate; they confirm standard Kaluza-Klein.

The test does confirm one real physics result: simple sphere geometries
(S², S³, CP²) cannot produce SM matter content alone. The CP² hole at
multiplicity 2 is the well-known reason SU(2) appears explicitly in SM.
But this is textbook physics, not §XIII.1 content.

CUMULATIVE FINDING across 4 spikes:

  #1 (PR #350 commit D)  Forward eigenvalue search: no fit
  #2 SG-decimation       Refuted directly (k_μ, k_τ not coherent across families)
  #3 Universal-b sweep   Diophantine-degenerate (null test rules out method)
  #4 Multiplicity test   Tautological when G/H = SM gauge group

Plus the "massive span" structural problem: no bounded fractal eigenvalue
spectrum natively spans 11 orders of magnitude. Either deep decimation
(k ~ 16 levels for b=5) or exponential mass formula (= Yukawa structure)
required, neither PREDICTED by §XIII.1.

HONEST VERDICT: §XIII.1 as currently articulated appears to be a
fractal-decorated Kaluza-Klein construction with no new predictive content
beyond standard KK. The fractal F adds eigenvalue layers but does not fix
the gauge group choice, the 11-order-of-magnitude mass span, or the
3-generation structure — each of these must be input separately.

This does NOT prove §XIII.1 has no answer. It proves the eigenvalue-based
search methods we've tried (forward, inverse-decimation, base-sweep,
multiplicity) cannot find one. A real framework would need testing on:
  - Running couplings / RG flow on the fractal
  - Topological invariants
  - Anomaly cancellation
  - Some yet-unidentified invariant that distinguishes "fractal × G/H"
    from "G/H alone with fractal decoration"

Per [[user_explanation_discipline]] + [[project_may8_commit_gemini_taint_caveat]]:
honest negative finding shipped, no patching to save the framework.
The remaining question is whether §XIII.1's articulation in the MFO
notebook has Gemini-CLI provenance that requires audit.

